00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:35,480
put 1% more into your pension before you

277
00:09:35,500 --> 00:09:36,450
get used to the money.

278
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:39,320
You'll barely notice it in your take-home,

279
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:41,400
and over a career it adds up to a very

280
00:09:41,420 --> 00:09:42,350
different retirement.

281
00:09:43,580 --> 00:09:45,780
Lever three is time, and it's the biggest.

282
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:48,600
Look at the same £300 a month,

283
00:09:48,620 --> 00:09:51,130
growing at 7% a year before inflation.

284
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,730
Over 35 years, it grows to about

285
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:55,440
£540,000.

286
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:57,850
Over 25 years, about

287
00:09:58,770 --> 00:09:59,570
£243,000.

288
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:02,360
Ten fewer years, and you end up with less

289
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:03,180
than half.

290
00:10:04,180 --> 00:10:05,690
And there's a quiet fourth lever:

291
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:09,450
fees. On £300 a month over 30 years,

292
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:11,600
the difference between paying 1% a year in

293
00:10:11,620 --> 00:10:14,530
fees and paying 0.3% is about

294
00:10:15,410 --> 00:10:16,210
£43,700.

295
00:10:16,680 --> 00:10:18,850
Same money in, same markets.

296
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:20,530
Just less going to someone else.

297
00:10:21,620 --> 00:10:22,550
And while you're checking:

298
00:10:22,680 --> 00:10:25,320
in 2024, the Pensions Policy Institute

299
00:10:25,340 --> 00:10:27,860
estimated there were about 3.3 million

300
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:29,380
lost pension pots in the UK,

301
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:32,510
holding about £31.1 billion.

302
00:10:33,760 --> 00:10:36,450
Old jobs, old addresses, forgotten pots.

303
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:38,460
If you've changed jobs a few times,

304
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:39,620
search for the government's Pension

305
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:40,790
Tracing Service.

306
00:10:41,020 --> 00:10:42,940
It's free, and some of that money might be

307
00:10:42,960 --> 00:10:43,760
yours.

308
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:45,780
And it doesn't need to be clever.

309
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:48,420
Most workplace pensions, and most people's

310
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:51,020
ISAs, are invested in funds like this one,

311
00:10:51,040 --> 00:10:52,710
spread across hundreds of companies.

312
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:55,090
No stock picking, no timing the market.

313
00:10:55,300 --> 00:10:57,440
Just money going in every month and years

314
00:10:57,460 --> 00:10:58,610
doing the heavy lifting.

315
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:01,930
So here's a five-minute job for this week.

316
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:03,750
Log in to your workplace pension.

317
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,360
Check how much is going in,

318
00:11:05,380 --> 00:11:06,910
from you and from your employer,

319
00:11:07,120 --> 00:11:08,980
what it's invested in, and what the fees

320
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,190
are. Most people have never looked.

321
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:12,980
The ones who look tend to put in more.

322
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,210
So So, back to the question in the title.

323
00:11:16,540 --> 00:11:18,280
Do you really need a million pounds to

324
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:19,100
retire?

325
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:21,870
For a comfortable retirement on your own,

326
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:23,010
yes, roughly.

327
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:26,500
You'd need a pot of about £1.05 million in

328
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:27,330
today's money.

329
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,190
But for a couple, it's about

330
00:11:29,930 --> 00:11:30,730
£587,000

331
00:11:31,060 --> 00:11:33,040
each, because you have two state pensions

332
00:11:33,060 --> 00:11:34,230
